What is the historical context of the British lease on Hong Kong, including the terms and duration of the lease?
The British lease on Hong Kong was established through the Second Convention of Peking in 1898, which included a 99-year lease agreement for the New Territories, a region surrounding Hong Kong. The lease awarded control of over 200 small islands to the British in exchange for a promise to return the islands to China after 99 years. The lease also included the rest of Kowloon south of the Shenzhen River and 230 islands, which became known as the New Territories. The British formally took possession on April 16, 1898.
