Identify the first permanent English settlement in the New World and its historical significance.
The first permanent English settlement in the New World was Jamestown, Virginia. Established in 1607, Jamestown was a pivotal historical event as it marked the beginning of English colonization in North America. It was the first successful English settlement in what is now the United States, preceding the Pilgrims' settlement at Plymouth, Massachusetts by 13 years. Jamestown played a significant role in the development of the English colonies in North America, serving as a model for future settlements and contributing to the growth of the English-speaking population in the region.
