Determine the specific roles and responsibilities of the lower house of the British parliament, including its relationship with the upper house and the monarch.
Based on the provided context, specifically the "Queen-in-Parliament" and "Westminster system" articles, here is the answer to the sub-question:

The lower house of the British parliament is the de facto legislative body, where bills are passed and sent to the monarch (or their representative) for Royal Assent, which makes the bill a law. The lower house has equal legislative responsibility with the upper house, but the upper house typically practices restraint and serves as a consultative body. The monarch, in turn, acts with the advice and consent of the parliament, including both the lower and upper houses, in their legislative role as the Crown-in-Parliament.
