What is the current definition and working mechanism of the joule as a unit of heat, and how does it differ from its predecessor?
The current definition of the joule as a unit of heat is the energy dissipated as heat when an electric current of one ampere passes through a resistance of one ohm for one second. 

The joule was previously defined in terms of base units, but it was later redefined in terms of the energy transferred to (or work done on) an object when a force of one newton acts on that object in the direction of its motion through a distance of one meter. 

The predecessor of the joule was the caloric theory, which considered heat as a fluid that could be transferred from one body to another. In contrast, the joule is based on the mechanical theory of heat, which views heat as a form of energy.
