How does the toxic compound in rhubarb leaves interact with the human body to cause poisoning?
The toxic compound in rhubarb leaves is oxalic acid, which can cause poisoning in humans. When ingested, oxalic acid can bind to calcium in the body, forming insoluble calcium oxalate crystals. This can lead to a range of symptoms, including kidney damage, kidney stones, and potentially life-threatening complications.
