What is the geological process of metamorphosis, and how does it relate to the formation of marble?
Metamorphism is the geological process of changing the mineral composition and structure of rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions, without melting them. In the case of marble, metamorphism involves the recrystallization of sedimentary carbonate rocks, such as limestone or dolomite, resulting in the formation of a new rock composed of an interlocking mosaic of carbonate crystals. This process causes the original textures and structures of the original rock to be modified or destroyed, resulting in the characteristic marble rock.
