What is the history of public libraries in the UK, specifically focusing on the establishment of the first public library?
The history of public libraries in the UK began with the establishment of the Francis Trigge Chained Library in Grantham, Lincolnshire, in 1598, which is considered the forerunner of later public library systems. However, the modern concept of free, open-access libraries started to take shape in the UK in 1847, when Parliament appointed a committee to consider the necessity of establishing libraries through the nation. The committee's report in 1849 noted the poor condition of library service and recommended the establishment of free public libraries. This led to the Public Libraries Act in 1850, which allowed cities with populations exceeding 10,000 to levy taxes for the support of public libraries. The first unconditionally free public library in England was Salford Museum and Art Gallery, which opened in November 1850.
